course: world
lesson: 114
title: Birthdays, ages and "how many sleeps"
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Little children count days to a big day in "sleeps": each night's sleep brings you one day closer. On 18 December, Christmas on the 25th is 25 − 18 = 7 sleeps away. In the same month, just take the smaller date from the bigger one.
explain: Across two months, count to the end of the first month, then add on. From 20 November: November has 30 days, so 10 sleeps take you to 30 November, and 25 more to Christmas: 10 + 25 = 35 sleeps. You must know how long the month is: from 28 March to 3 April is 3 sleeps to 31 March (March has 31 days) and 3 more, 6 in all.
explain: Your age goes up by one on each birthday. This year minus your birth year gives the age you reach on this year's birthday: Charlie, born in December 2016, is 2026 − 2016 = 10 only after his December birthday; before that he is still 9. Turn it round to find a birth year: Grandma turning 80 in 2026 was born in 2026 − 80 = 1946.
explain: An age gap never changes, because both people get older at the same speed. Ruby is 9 and her brother is 6, a gap of 3 years, so when Ruby is 20 he will be 17, not 10.
explain: Age is also a rule people agree on. In the old Korean way a baby was 1 at birth and everyone got a year older on New Year's Day, so people could be up to 2 years "older" than in the international way; in 2023 South Korea made the international way official for most things.
fact: "Sleeps" means the nights left until a special day.
fact: For two dates in the same month, take the smaller date from the bigger: from 18 to 25 December is 7 sleeps.
fact: From 20 November to 25 December is 35 sleeps: 10 to the end of November, which has 30 days, and 25 in December.
fact: From 28 March to 3 April is 6 sleeps: 3 to the end of March, which has 31 days, and 3 in April.
fact: An age gap between two people never changes: if Ruby is 9 and her brother 6, the gap is always 3 years.
fact: This year minus your birth year gives your age after this year's birthday; before your birthday you are one year younger.
fact: To find a birth year, take the age from the year of that birthday: 2026 − 80 = 1946.
fact: Your birthday is the date you were born, coming round again each year: Tia, born on 12 September 2016, turned 10 on 12 September 2026.
fact: In the old Korean way of counting, a baby was 1 at birth and everyone got a year older on New Year's Day; South Korea made the international way of counting age official in 2023.
fact: A half-birthday is 6 months after your birthday, because a year has 12 months: 10 January plus 6 months is 10 July.
fact: 10 years is about 3,650 days, plus 2 or 3 extra leap days, so about 3,652 or 3,653 days.
fact: Some people celebrate their 10,000th day of life, which comes when they are a little over 27 years old.
fact: Words people use: "turn 10" means have your 10th birthday, "bussin'" means really tasty (young US slang), "bostin'" means great and "bab" is a friendly word like "love" (Black Country, England), "tara-a-bit" means bye for now (Black Country) and "wee" means small (Scotland).
exercise: It is 18 December. How many sleeps until Christmas on the 25th? | 7 | 8 | 6 | 43
exercise: It is 20 November. How many sleeps until Christmas? | 35 | 5 | 45 | 25
exercise: How many days does November have? | 30 | 31 | 28 | 29
exercise: Ruby is 9 and her brother is 6. When Ruby is 20, how old will he be? | 17 | 10 | 14 | 23
exercise: What happens to the age gap between two people as they grow up? | It stays the same | It gets bigger every year | It gets smaller every year | It disappears when they are adults
exercise: Charlie was born in December 2016. How old is he in June 2026? | 9 | 10 | 11 | 16
exercise: Grandma has her 80th birthday in July 2026. In what year was she born? | 1946 | 1956 | 1936 | 2106
exercise: On 12 September 2026 Tia's mum says, "Ten years ago today." When was Tia born? | 12 September 2016 | 12 September 2006 | 12 September 2010 | 2 September 2016
exercise: Jacob's birthday is 3 April. It is 28 March. How many sleeps? | 6 | 3 | 5 | 9
exercise: Why must you know how long March is to count from 28 March to 3 April? | To know how many days are left before April starts | To know which day of the week it is | Because March has no 29th or 30th | Because April starts on 30 March
exercise: In the old Korean way, how old was a newborn baby? | 1 | 0 | 2 | 12
exercise: What did South Korea do in 2023? | Made the international way of counting age official | Made everyone one year older at once | Moved all birthdays to New Year's Day | Stopped people celebrating birthdays
exercise: Ellie's birthday is 10 January. When is her half-birthday? | 10 July | 10 June | 10 August | 5 January
exercise: About how many days have you lived when you are 10? | About 3,650 | About 365 | About 36,500 | About 1,000
exercise: About how old are you on your 10,000th day? | A little over 27 | Exactly 10 years old | About 100 years old | About 50 years old
test: Ava on 20 November: "Christmas is like 5 sleeps, it's bussin'!" Put her right. | It's 35 sleeps: 10 left in November and 25 in December | It's 5 sleeps, she's right | It's 45 sleeps: 30 and 15 | It's 25 sleeps: just December
test: Ruby boasts, "When I'm 20, our kid will be 10, half my age." Her brother is 6 now and she's 9. What's the truth? | He'll be 17; the 3-year gap never changes | He'll be 10; the gap halves as you grow up | He'll be 20 too; younger ages always catch up | He'll be 14; the gap shrinks a bit every year
test: Charlie's mate: "Born 2016, it's 2026, so you're 10, innit?" Charlie: "Not till December." Who's right in June? | Charlie; he's still 9 until his birthday | The mate; he's 10 for the whole of the year | Neither; he's 11 | Neither; he's 8
test: Ayaan, working out Grandma's 80th: "2026 plus 80, she was born in 2106!" What went wrong? | He should take 80 away: she was born in 1946 | He's right; she was born in 2106 | He should take 26 away: so she was born in 1980 | He should add 20: 2046
test: Jacob counts sleeps from 28 March to 3 April as "3 April minus 28 March, that's minus 25, eh?" What's the real answer? | 6 sleeps, counting to the end of March first | 25 sleeps | 3 sleeps | 31 sleeps
test: Min-jun's grandma laughs, "In the old way I'm 2 years older!" How can that be? | The old Korean way counted 1 at birth and added a year at New Year | Korean years are shorter than other years | She lied about her age for 2 years | She was born twice in two countries
test: A Black Country nan: "Bostin' cake for your half-birthday, bab!" Ellie's birthday is 10 January. When is the half-birthday cake? | 10 July | 10 June | 10 December | 5 July
test: Liam: "I'm 10, so I've lived exactly 3,650 days, no more, no less." What did he leave out? | The 2 or 3 extra leap days | The weekends, which do not count | The days before he could walk | Nothing; it is exactly 3,650
test: What would happen to Ruby's and her brother's ages if both had a birthday tomorrow? | Both would go up by 1, and the gap would stay 3 | Only Ruby's age would go up | The gap would become 4 years | The gap would become 2 years
test: Tom on 18 December, wee and excited: "Seven sleeps or eight?" Christmas is the 25th. | 7 | 8 | 6 | 25
test: A cartoon cat says, "My birthday comes once in my life." Put it right. | A birthday comes round again every year on the same date | A birthday only happens in leap years | A birthday comes every 4 years for everyone | A birthday only happens on your first day
exam: It is given that the present date is 20 November. Determine the number of nights remaining until 25 December. | Thirty-five | Five | Forty-five | Twenty-five
exam: Whereas one sibling is aged nine and another six, ascertain the age of the younger upon the elder attaining twenty years. | Seventeen | Ten | Fourteen | Twenty-three
exam: A person celebrates the eightieth anniversary of birth in the year 2026. State the year of birth. | 1946 | 1956 | 1936 | 2106
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the reckoning of age in South Korea since 2023. | The international method has been made official for most purposes | Every citizen is counted as one year old at birth | All birthdays are celebrated on New Year's Day | The reckoning of age has been abolished
